                        HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ



Under my palms, the stone ring picked it up and started to hum, and
the clearing’s whole sound turned a degree toward me and locked.
    The clearing in 1991. The oaks were smaller, which I hadn’t known
they’d ever been. A young man with his hair cut short sat on this exact
stone with a palo drum between his knees and played to a clearing that
was mostly trash and broken glass, with six people in it and four of
them children. A woman of about thirty-five sat on a plastic crate be‐
side him working a shekere, and I knew the angle of her jaw.
    Nobody was in the park to hear it. That wasn’t the part that
mattered.
    At the edge of the path, where the oaks gave way to the lot, a man
stood with his back against a tree. Short sleeves, a tie, a small notebook.
He wasn’t listening to the music. He was watching the people. He
counted the kids, then the adults, then the kids again. He wrote down
a number, and the time, and when a police car idled at the curb and
rolled on, he wrote that down too. After ten minutes he ticked some‐
thing, closed the book, and walked off toward the avenue, satisfied.
    I stopped breathing. It was a different man from the one in the café,
the one in the window. Same notebook. Same habit. They had been
counting this circle for thirty-five years.
    I came back with a start, with sweat running down my back and a
shaker in my fist. The circle was winding down, Rafa bringing it home
with the same invisible authority he’d started it with, and I was laugh‐
ing. I had no idea how long it had been.
    Manny was looking at me across the ring like a proud older brother.
Yenni smiled her corner smile. Rafa set the drum aside and looked at
me for a long moment with those carved hands resting on his knees.
    “You have questions,” he said. “I can see them stacking up behind
your face.” He stood, slowly, the way a man stands when his knees
have been folded for two hours and sixty-some years. “You won’t get
them all answered today. But I’ll answer one. Pick the real one. Not the
safe one. I’ll know.”
